  "body": "## What was broken\n\n`src/my_claude_code/providers/chatgpt_oauth/` ignored the reasoning policy completely. A client sending `thinking: {type: \"disabled\"}`, or a route configured `reasoning=off`, still got reasoning at effort `\"medium\"` — on **100% of chatgpt_oauth traffic**. This provider serves `MODEL_VISION` and the first `MODEL_FALLBACK`, so it was live on every vision request.\n\n## Mechanism\n\n`build_chatgpt_oauth_request_body` had **no reasoning parameter at all**:\n\n```python\ndef build_chatgpt_oauth_request_body(\n    request: MessagesRequest,\n    *,\n    default_max_tokens: int | None = None,\n) -> dict[str, Any]:\n```\n\nand both call sites in `provider.py` (`preflight_stream` ~183, `stream_response` ~232) dropped the fully-gated `ReasoningPolicy` they were handed:\n\n```python\nbuild_chatgpt_oauth_request_body(request)\n```\n\nso the reasoning block was a constant, emitted whenever a private helper matched the model id by string prefix:\n\n```python\ndef _supports_reasoning(model: str) -> bool:\n    name = model.lower()\n    return name.startswith(\"gpt-5\") or name.startswith(\"codex\") or name.startswith(\"o\")\n```\n\n`startswith(\"o\")` matches **any** id beginning with the letter \"o\", and model-name branching for reasoning capability is explicitly forbidden by the project's architecture principles.\n\n## What the fix does\n\n- Adds a **required keyword-only `reasoning: ReasoningPolicy`** to `build_chatgpt_oauth_request_body`, and passes the real policy from **both** call sites.\n- Encodes the policy in the Responses API dialect:\n  - `ReasoningControl.OFF` → **no** `reasoning` block and **no** `include` key. Omission is the safe encoding; `effort: \"none\"` is not documented for every model this backend serves.\n  - A named effort → `{\"effort\": <mapped>, \"summary\": \"auto\"}`.\n  - Provider-default / unspecified → `effort: \"medium\"`, preserving today's behaviour so no user's default silently changes.\n  - Whenever a block is emitted, `include: [\"reasoning.encrypted_content\"]` is kept — it is what makes stateless multi-turn reasoning work under `store: false`.\n- Deletes `_supports_reasoning` and its call.\n\n### Capability decision: rely on upstream gating (option b)\n\nNot a models.dev lookup inside the encoder. The policy reaching a provider has **already** passed through `application/reasoning_gating.py::adapt_reasoning_policy`, which suppresses reasoning for models known not to support it and clamps efforts to the model's published vocabulary. Re-testing capability in the encoder would duplicate that decision and create a second source of truth. This also matches the project's layering: capability gating belongs to the application layer, wire encoding to the provider. No new name-prefix test was introduced.\n\n### Effort mapping\n\n| `ReasoningEffort` | sent as | note |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `minimal` | `minimal` | verbatim |\n| `low` | `low` | verbatim |\n| `medium` | `medium` | verbatim |\n| `high` | `high` | verbatim |\n| `xhigh` | `high` | **mapped down** — the Responses endpoint documents no named effort above `high` |\n| `max` | `high` | **mapped down**, same reason |\n| *(no effort named)* | `medium` | preserved default |\n| `control=OFF` | *(block omitted)* | no `reasoning`, no `include` |\n\nThe narrowing mirrors `_LOW_MEDIUM_HIGH` in `providers/openai_chat/profiles.py`, which solves the same problem for chat-completions providers with a shorter vocabulary.\n\n### Deliberately unchanged\n\n`default_max_tokens` still sends no explicit output limit to the Codex/Responses endpoint — that is a separate concern and out of scope here.\n\n## Tests\n\nAdded to `tests/providers/test_chatgpt_oauth.py`:\n\n- `test_build_request_body_keeps_medium_for_the_provider_default_policy`\n- `test_build_request_body_omits_reasoning_and_include_when_off`\n- `test_build_request_body_encodes_each_named_effort` (parametrized over all six efforts)\n- `test_build_request_body_keeps_encrypted_content_whenever_reasoning_is_sent`\n- `test_build_request_body_no_longer_reasons_on_a_model_id_starting_with_o` — the named regression: `startswith(\"o\")` is gone\n- `test_preflight_stream_passes_the_policy_to_the_body_builder`\n- `test_stream_response_passes_the_policy_to_the_body_builder` — these two guard against the original bug shape (parameter added but never passed)\n\n## Verification\n\n`.\\scripts\\ci.ps1` — suppression grep, `ruff format`, `ruff check --fix` and **repo-wide** `ty check` all clean.\n\nTargeted suite:\n\n```\n$ uv run pytest tests/providers/test_chatgpt_oauth.py -q\n..........................................                               [100%]\n42 passed in 7.86s\n```\n\nFull suite:\n\n```\n$ uv run pytest -q\n14 failed, 5256 passed, 38 skipped in 83.86s (0:01:23)\n```\n\nThe local suite has **14 pre-existing failures that also fail on unmodified `main`** — 8 websearch / `test_web_server_tools` tests that read the real `~/.fcc/.env`, and 6 PowerShell installer tests. None are in files this PR touches, and the count is unchanged.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **`xhigh`/`max` narrowing is a judgement call.** They are mapped to `high` rather than sent verbatim, checked against the published Responses API effort vocabulary, **not against a live ChatGPT/Codex OAuth key**. If the backend later accepts a higher named effort, this table is the one place to widen.\n- **A provider-default policy still sends `effort: \"medium\"`.** That is deliberate (preserving current behaviour), but it means a hypothetical non-reasoning model that upstream gating did not catch — capability `None`, i.e. no models.dev row — would still receive a reasoning block. In practice the provider's own allowlist admits only GPT-5.4+/Codex models, all of which reason.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_016Rsc8AeKxx8h6zFwfT9sD4\n",
