    },
    "message": "fix(chatgpt_oauth): honour the reasoning policy instead of hardcoding medium\n\nThe ChatGPT/Codex OAuth provider ignored the reasoning policy entirely. A\nclient sending `thinking: {type: \"disabled\"}`, or a route configured\n`reasoning=off`, still got reasoning at effort \"medium\" on every request --\n100% of chatgpt_oauth traffic, which serves MODEL_VISION and the first\nMODEL_FALLBACK here.\n\nMechanism: `build_chatgpt_oauth_request_body` had no reasoning parameter at\nall, and both call sites in `provider.py` (`preflight_stream`,\n`stream_response`) dropped the fully-gated `ReasoningPolicy` they were\nhanded. The body builder therefore emitted a constant\n`{\"effort\": \"medium\", \"summary\": \"auto\"}` block whenever a private\n`_supports_reasoning(model)` helper matched the model id -- by string prefix\n(`gpt-5`, `codex`, or bare `o`, so any id starting with the letter \"o\"),\nwhich is exactly the model-name branching the project forbids.\n\nThe fix adds a required keyword-only `reasoning: ReasoningPolicy` to the\nbody builder and passes the real policy from both call sites. Encoding:\n`ReasoningControl.OFF` emits no `reasoning` block and no `include` key at\nall (omission is accepted everywhere; `effort: \"none\"` is not documented for\nevery model this backend serves); a named effort emits\n`{\"effort\": <mapped>, \"summary\": \"auto\"}`; an unspecified policy keeps\n\"medium\" so nobody's default changes. Whenever a block is emitted,\n`include: [\"reasoning.encrypted_content\"]` is kept -- it is what makes\nstateless multi-turn reasoning work under `store: false`.\n\n`_supports_reasoning` is deleted rather than replaced with a models.dev\nlookup. Capability gating is the application layer's job: the policy\nreaching a provider has already been through\n`application/reasoning_gating.py::adapt_reasoning_policy`, which suppresses\nreasoning for models known not to support it. Re-testing capability inside\nthe encoder would duplicate that decision and re-introduce a second source\nof truth.\n\nEffort mapping (FCC vocabulary -> Responses API): minimal, low, medium and\nhigh pass through verbatim; xhigh and max map down to \"high\", because the\nendpoint documents no named effort above \"high\". This mirrors\n`_LOW_MEDIUM_HIGH` in `providers/openai_chat/profiles.py`, which solves the\nsame narrowing for chat-completions providers.\n\n`default_max_tokens` behaviour is unchanged: the Codex/Responses endpoint\nstill receives no explicit output limit.\n\nVerification: `.\\scripts\\ci.ps1` -- suppression grep, ruff format, ruff\ncheck --fix and repo-wide `ty check` all clean; `uv run pytest -q` reports\n`14 failed, 5256 passed, 38 skipped`, which is exactly the known baseline on\nunmodified main (8 websearch tests that read the real ~/.fcc/.env, 6\nPowerShell installer tests). `tests/providers/test_chatgpt_oauth.py` alone:\n42 passed.\n\nNot done: sending `max_tokens` upstream (separate concern), and any change\nto how gating decides capability.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016Rsc8AeKxx8h6zFwfT9sD4",
